{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-07-counting-digits",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Counting Digits with Logarithms",
    "description": "Use the characteristic of a common logarithm to find how many digits large powers and products contain.",
    "teachingApproach": "Connect digit count directly to powers of 10, then use common logs to place huge powers between two consecutive powers of 10. Use supplied log values only, keep multiplication visible, and make the final characteristic-to-digits step explicit every time.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10, Logarithms. Teach that a positive number N has floor(log N)+1 digits, equivalently one more than the characteristic of log N. Apply this to powers using log(a^n)=n log a and to products by adding logarithms. Cover chapter-style examples using supplied values of log2 and log3, including powers of 2, 4, 5, and 6 and products such as powers of 4 and 5. Make the final step explicit: a logarithm between k and k+1 means the number has k+1 digits."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's use logarithms to count digits in numbers that are too large to expand.\n\nThe idea is simple: find which two powers of 10 the number lies between.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If log N=5.42, then:\n\n10^5 < N < 10^6.\n\nSo N has 6 digits.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For any positive integer N:\n\nnumber of digits = floor(log N)+1.\n\nIn chapter language, it is characteristic+1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
